@database "message_debugger"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: message_debugger.guide 1.0 (29.09.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "Message Debugger"
@{h1}Message Debugger

What did the agent actually send to its model, and what came back? The
Message Debugger records it for every LLM call: the messages of each step
as the model gets them (a @{i}turn@{ui}), and the raw request to the
provider with its response, usage, duration and error. It writes them into
a database on the side, without slowing the agent down, and shows them in
the @{b}Message Debugger@{ub} panel -- each user their own calls, an admin
everyone's.

@{image docs/panel.png "The Message Debugger panel with the turns of a request"}

@{code}
For users
  @{"  The Message Debugger panel  " link panel}
  @{"  Who sees what  " link access}

For agents and developers
  @{"  The capture hooks  " link hooks}
  @{"  Size and retention  " link retention}
  @{"  The HTTP API  " link api}
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}
@{body}
@endnode

@node panel "The Message Debugger panel"
@{h1}The Message Debugger panel

Open it from the launcher (@{b}Debugging@{ub}, "Message Debugger"). From
the chat it opens on one thing: click an answer's request id, or the
session id in the header, and pick it under "Open in". Opened that way,
both lists start filtered to that request or session. The panel does not
follow the chat; the filters say what it shows.

@{image docs/panel.png "The Message Debugger panel"}

@{h2}The figures

Turns, LLM requests, sessions, agents and errors among the captures you
may see (see @{" Who sees what " link access}); an admin also sees the size
of the database file. @{b}Errors@{ub} counts the responses that carry an
error, retried attempts included. @{b}Sessions@{ub} is the number of
sessions of the turns or of the requests, whichever is larger -- not the
two together.

@{h2}The two lists

@{bullet}@{b}Turns@{ub}: one row per snapshot -- @{i}input@{ui}, the
messages before the call, or @{i}output@{ui}, after it with the model's
answer. Time, agent, step, number of messages, their estimated tokens, the
share of the prompt read from the provider's cache and the cost (both from
the answer's usage, so only on output rows), session and request.
@{bullet}@{b}LLM requests@{ub}: the raw calls -- @{i}request@{ui} rows
with the payload, @{i}response@{ui} rows with duration, tokens, cost and a
status: the finish reason, @{i}error@{ui} with its text, or
@{i}retry n/m@{ui} for an attempt that failed and was tried again. The
model column names the backend a gateway routed the call to ("via ...")
and marks streamed calls.

Both come newest first, 50 at a time; @{b}Load more@{ub} brings 50 more,
up to the newest 500 the filters match -- for older entries, narrow the
filters. Each tab counts every entry its filters match. A click on a
column head sorts the loaded entries by it.

@{h2}Filters

Agent, session id, request id, and for an admin a user; on the turns tab
input or output, on the requests tab the provider and requests or
responses. A request id takes the calls under it along: its tool calls
(@{tt}<id>_001@{utt}) and sub-agents (@{tt}<id>_sub_...@{utt}), shown in
the request column by what follows the id. A session shows its own calls;
a sub-agent's are in its own session.

@{h2}Refreshing

The toolbar's refresh button loads what was captured since; the menu next
to it refreshes every few seconds instead. Off -- as the panel starts
until you pick a rate, which it remembers -- the lists hold still at the last refresh: a page more, another tab or
other filters show nothing captured after it.

@{h2}An entry

A click on a row -- or Enter or Space -- opens it in a drawer:

@{bullet}a turn: its facts and the messages as cards -- text as text, a
JSON tool result and tool arguments as trees, every other field of the
message by name -- and for output the model's answer;
@{bullet}a request or response: its facts, the error, the usage, the
request payload and the response data.

The messages, usage, payload and response data have a copy button
(JSON), the error one for its text. The arrows step to the next newer or
older entry of the list; the filter button next to the session or request
narrows both lists to it.

@{h2}Maintenance (admins)

The menu at the right of the toolbar:

@{table}
Item | Does
Prune and reclaim disk space | shrinks the data below the size cap (see @{" Size and retention " link retention}), then compacts the file; reports what it did
Clear all captured data | deletes every turn and request, after asking -- cost history included
@{body}

On a large database either takes minutes. Captures this server makes
meanwhile wait in its queue (up to @{tt}capture_queue_max@{utt}, beyond
that the newest are dropped) and are written after it; those of other
processes writing the same file -- agent-cli runs, workers -- are lost.
When the server refuses -- a list or entry you may not see, a panel of
someone not signed in -- the panel shows its reason in place of the list,
or in the drawer for an entry.
@endnode

@node access "Who sees what"
@{h1}Who sees what

Every capture carries the user whose call it was: the user the run's
session was opened for, else the user its request was registered under. A
call no run names a user for -- a TTS or decision call outside a run --
and everything captured before captures had owners belong to nobody.

@{table}
Who | Sees
A signed-in user | their own captures, made since their account was created; the figures without the file's size
An admin | everyone's, nobody's included; the user filter narrows to one user
Everyone, with authentication off | what an admin sees
Not signed in (anonymous), agent-cli's cli_user | nothing: "Sign in to see the captures of your own runs."
@{body}

Another user's entry, opened by its number, answers "not found", as one
that does not exist. Naming another user is refused ("Another user's
captures are for admins."), and so are pruning and clearing, which act on
everyone's captures.

@{h2}Known gaps

@{bullet}Entry numbers count over everyone's captures: from the gaps
between their own, a user sees how many calls others made in between, and
roughly when -- never what.
@{bullet}An owner is a name. @{tt}agent-cli --session-user NAME@{utt}
records under a name that need not be an account, and whoever registers
that name later reads what is captured under it from then on.
@endnode

@node hooks "The capture hooks"
@{h1}The capture hooks

Four hooks, all on for every agent whose hooks are on:

@{table}
Hook | Runs | Records
debugger_capture_pre_llm | before each LLM call, after the other hooks have changed the history | an input turn: the messages
debugger_capture_post_llm | after each LLM call | an output turn: the same messages and the model's answer
debugger_capture_pre_request | in the LLM client, before the request goes out | the raw payload, provider, model, URL, streaming
debugger_capture_post_response | in the LLM client, when the call ends -- also failed and retried | response data, usage, duration, finish reason, error, the backend that served it
@{body}

Calls made outside an agent -- text-to-speech, decision models -- report
their requests and responses too.

@{h2}What a turn keeps

Every field of every message, so a field added later shows up without a
change here. @{tt}content@{utt} and @{tt}tool_calls@{utt} are kept whole;
any other string longer than @{tt}max_field_chars@{utt} is cut to a
preview that names its length ("... [5000 chars]") -- encrypted reasoning
or base64 media would otherwise repeat in every snapshot of a run. The
model's answer in an output turn is cut the same way, its text included:
the whole answer is in the response entry. Each message gets its index,
its length, with @{tt}include_token_estimates@{utt} an estimate of its
tokens and, with tool calls on, their
number and whether it is a tool result.

@{h2}What they change

Nothing: the hooks only read. The model sees no difference, and neither
its tokens nor the prompt cache are touched. A hook hands its capture to a
background writer and returns at once; a slow database makes the queue
lag, never the agent. When the queue is full (@{tt}capture_queue_max@{utt})
the newest captures are dropped, and the log says how many.

@{h2}Switching them off

For all agents, in the plugin's server entry (see @{" Setting it up " link setup}):
@{tt}capture_enabled: false@{utt}, or one of the parts. For one agent, in
its YAML:

@{code yaml}
agent_config:
  hooks:
    overrides:
      message_debugger.debugger_capture_pre_llm:
        enabled: false
      message_debugger.debugger_capture_post_llm:
        enabled: false
@{body}

The settings are read from the server entry only; an agent's override can
switch a hook off, but not change its settings. An agent with
@{tt}hooks.enabled: false@{utt} is not captured at all.
@endnode

@node retention "Size and retention"
@{h1}Size and retention

The database keeps its data below @{tt}max_db_size_mb@{utt} (5120 MB).
Every 200 writes it measures the data; above about 90 % it works down to
about 75 %, in slices of about a second:

@{bullet}the oldest raw payloads and responses are emptied -- the row with
provider, model, usage, request id and times stays, so cost reports keep
working;
@{bullet}the oldest turns are deleted;
@{bullet}only when nothing else is left, the oldest request rows go.

The file stops growing at its largest size and reuses the freed space; it
does not shrink. @{b}Prune and reclaim disk space@{ub} in the panel runs
the same down to 75 %, for at most two minutes (the automatic retention
finishes the rest), and then compacts the file (VACUUM), which
gives the space back to the disk -- also the space a clear freed. The
compaction needs free disk space about the size of the remaining data; if
it fails, the panel says so. @{tt}max_db_size_mb: 0@{utt} turns retention
off; the prune then only compacts.
@endnode

@node api "The HTTP API"
@{h1}The HTTP API

Under @{tt}/plugins/message_debugger/@{utt} (the instance name), with the
rules of @{" Who sees what " link access}:

@{table}
Route | Answers
GET turns | turns without their messages: total, turns, as_of_id
GET turns/{id} | one turn with messages and answer
GET llm-requests | request log entries without payload and response: total, requests, as_of_id
GET llm-requests/{id} | one entry, whole
GET stats | the figures; for an admin also db_size_mb
POST prune?vacuum=true | admins: retention down to 75 %, then VACUUM
DELETE clear | admins: deletes everything
GET / | the panel
@{body}

The lists take @{tt}agent_name@{utt}, @{tt}session_id@{utt},
@{tt}request_id@{utt} (with the calls under it),
@{tt}snapshot_type@{utt} (turns: pre_llm, post_llm) or
@{tt}direction@{utt} and @{tt}provider@{utt} (requests),
@{tt}user_id@{utt} (admins), @{tt}limit@{utt} (1 to 500, default 50) and
@{tt}offset@{utt}. @{tt}as_of_id@{utt} is the newest entry the answer was
made as of; passed back as @{tt}max_id@{utt}, a list and its total leave
out what was captured since. Numbers only grow, also across a clear.
A turn row carries the usage of its answer as @{tt}usage_json@{utt}.
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

The plugin is on in the default configuration:

@{code yaml}
plugins:
  servers:
    message_debugger:
      type: message_debugger
      enabled: true
      config:
        max_db_size_mb: 5120
@{body}

It has no tools; no allowlist entry is needed. The hooks run for every
agent with hooks on.

@{h2}Server settings

All under @{tt}config:@{utt} in the server entry:

@{table}
Key | Default | Meaning
capture_enabled | true | false: capture nothing
capture_pre_llm | true | input turns
capture_post_llm | true | output turns
capture_llm_requests | true | raw requests and responses
include_tool_calls | true | false: turns leave out tool calls and tool call ids
include_token_estimates | true | false: no token estimate per message
max_field_chars | 500 | preview length of long fields in a turn; 0 keeps everything
db_path | empty | the database file; empty: message_debugger/debugger.db in the data directory
max_db_size_mb | 5120 | the size cap; 0: no retention
capture_queue_max | 2000 | captures waiting for the writer before the newest are dropped; 0: no limit
@{body}

The settings are read when the server starts. The database is one SQLite
file with its @{tt}-wal@{utt} and @{tt}-shm@{utt} files beside it. Other
tools, cost reports among them, read it directly: clearing it deletes
their history too.
@endnode
